          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A screening mammogram in a 55-year-old woman reveals clustered microcalcificatio</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A screening mammogram in a 55-year-old woman reveals clustered microcalcifications without a palpable lump. Which pathology is most commonly associated with this finding?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Invasive lobular carcinoma</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Invasive lobular carcinoma tends to be multifocal and sometimes bilateral, but it often lacks characteristic microcalcifications, making mammographic detection more difficult.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Ductal carcinoma in situ</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. DCIS is a premalignant lesion that commonly presents as microcalcifications on mammography, often detected on screening before a palpable lump develops.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Fibroadenoma</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Fibroadenomas may show coarse 'popcorn' calcifications on mammography, but clustered microcalcifications in a screening mammogram are the hallmark of a different pathology.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Fat necrosis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Fat necrosis can produce calcifications and mimic cancer, but it typically follows a history of breast trauma or surgery, which is absent in this screening-detected finding.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Clustered microcalcifications on screening mammography are most commonly associated with ductal carcinoma in situ (DCIS), a premalignant lesion that can progress to invasive cancer if untreated."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! Clustered microcalcifications on screening mammography are the hallmark of DCIS. Left untreated, DCIS can progress to invasive ductal carcinoma."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
